I was born in the high snow, a thread of cold light,
I learnt to run downhill before I learnt to sing;
I carried the mountains' secrets day and night
and gave them to the plains in the green of spring.

I fed your fields; I turned your mills;
I carried your boats with patient strength;
I listened to your songs and your quarrels,
and kept my promises along my length.

But now you pour your waste into my veins,
you dig my sand, you choke my mouth with stone;
my fish grow few; the heron leaves the plains;
I reach the sea exhausted and alone.

Remember me. I am older than your oldest name.
Give back my breath, and I will give you back the same.

by Rabindranath Tagore, from Gitanjali (1912)

Where the mind is without fear and the head is held high;
Where knowledge is free;
Where the world has not been broken up into fragments by narrow domestic walls;
Where words come out from the depth of truth;
Where tireless striving stretches its arms towards perfection;
Where the clear stream of reason has not lost its way into the dreary desert sand of dead habit;
Where the mind is led forward by thee into ever-widening thought and action —
Into that heaven of freedom, my Father, let my country awake.
